{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1105614-neo-versions","id":1105614,"title":"Neo Versions","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-06-19T23:25:11-07:00","id":1105614,"title":"Neo Versions","content":"I have seen it mentioned that neo paganism in Europe is somehow nothing like how it was 2 millenium before. That due to constant migration, displacement, and exchange of ideas, that the original pagan paths are lost and can never be truly recovered.<br /> <br /> Indeed, one can say something similar about the way of Rujia (Confucianism for those that want the latinization), which has been reinterpreted over the eons to what can be seen of it today in China and other places. Traditionalists will insist it is one way, and visionaries will try to move things in a different direction, but things always end up changing one way or another simply because a cycle of life and death exists.<br /> <br /> However ,although change in thought through the lens of myth and legend is always present, it isn't always fair. A friend of mine who is from the Phillipines, for example, never gets to play anything resembling his culture in international popular media (including tabletop rpgs) because so many influences from China, India, Arabia, and Spain have infiltrated their stories, its all just so wonderfully messy. That messiness unfortunately means that no one is interested in using it because of how difficult it is to parse out the influences (something I have recently discovered when looking into their stories). I personally like messy ideas and stories even though I hate colonialism, but it is very curious to me what is considered an authentic path that has not lost its identity and what is very much a &quot;neo&quot; version of a particular belief. After all, the largest difference seems to be dependent on how much it has changed. The less it has changed over time, the more authentic it is. In the same way, oral legends and myths are relatively humbled by the much more prolific written and documented legends and myths, as if the latter was superior to the former.<br /> <br /> So, what do you do? How do you decide whether to go for the 'authentic' versions of a myth or the 'neo' versions of the myth? Or something else?<br /> <br /> And before you answer, consider this:<br /> <br /> Scion, as a game about myths and legends, is retelling those stories once again, which means it falls under the 'Neo' no matter how accurately they try to replicate everything. Because they are humans bound by the cycle of life and death.<br /> <br /> Just a thought.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>How can I know if what I claim I know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I might not know? How can I know if what I claim I don't know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I do know?<br /> -Zhuangzi","edited":{"userid":1715,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1715-ostarion","user":"Ostarion","date":". 06-20-2017, 05:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1715-ostarion","name":"Ostarion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1715_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"305"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T01:24:37-07:00","id":1105633,"title":"","content":"I'm not sure the Ancient Egyptians would appreciate me recontextualizing their myths as transgender narratives amd that's not going to stop me.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":9}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T02:16:19-07:00","id":1105637,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1105614-neo-versions?p=1105633#post1105633\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure the Ancient Egyptians would appreciate me recontextualizing their myths as transgender narratives amd that's not going to stop me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Terse, and to the point. Fortunately you will never fully know.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>How can I know if what I claim I know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I might not know? How can I know if what I claim I don't know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I do know?<br /> -Zhuangzi","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1715-ostarion","name":"Ostarion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1715_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"305"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T09:48:06-07:00","id":1105737,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>atamajakki</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1105614-neo-versions?p=1105633#post1105633\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not sure the Ancient Egyptians would appreciate me recontextualizing their myths as transgender narratives amd that's not going to stop me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hell fucking yeah.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Developer for Exalted<br /> <br /> Want to write for Exalted? Look at the <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/submission-guidelines/\" target=\"_blank\">freelancer submission guidelines.</a><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/user?u=451515\" target=\"_blank\">Robert Vance's Patreon</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/527-robert-vance","name":"Robert Vance","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10044"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T09:59:22-07:00","id":1105745,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ostarion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1105614-neo-versions#post1105614\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">t is. In the same way, oral legends and myths are relatively humbled by the much more prolific written and documented legends and myths, as if the latter was superior to the former.<br /> <br /> So, what do you do? How do you decide whether to go for the 'authentic' versions of a myth or the 'neo' versions of the myth? Or something else?<br /> <br /> And before you answer, consider this:<br /> <br /> Scion, as a game about myths and legends, is retelling those stories once again, which means it falls under the 'Neo' no matter how accurately they try to replicate everything. Because they are humans bound by the cycle of life and death.<br /> <br /> Just a thought.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wrote up a whole post about this, but I actually realized I might be misunderstanding something. When you say that by retelling these stories they are 'Neo' in the sense that they are simply new, or 'Neo' in the sense of 'Neo-Paganism'? I realized the differentiation between those might be a tad confusing, but Neo-Paganism is <i>more</i> than simply being modern versions of those religions, they are revivals, reconstructions, and recontextualizations, not simply 'new' ones. (In fact, some Neo-Pagans do not like the suggestion that they are 'New' in any way, their belief being that they are 100% accurately following ancient beliefs.) The name can be very confusing if you are ESL, there is a whole <i>wild</i> history to the development of the Neo-Pagan movements, and the name can be the source of a lot of confusion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T10:34:27-07:00","id":1105757,"title":"","content":"Everybody can interpret ancient religions as they please at their table, there is plenty of room for individual freedom. Everybody has an individual take on religions as it should be.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T11:51:40-07:00","id":1105786,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ostarion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1105614-neo-versions#post1105614\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I have seen it mentioned that neo paganism in Europe is somehow nothing like how it was 2 millenium before. That due to constant migration, displacement, and exchange of ideas, that the original pagan paths are lost and can never be truly recovered.<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> I personally like messy ideas and stories even though I hate colonialism, but it is very curious to me what is considered an authentic path that has not lost its identity and what is very much a &quot;neo&quot; version of a particular belief. After all, the largest difference seems to be dependent on how much it has changed. The less it has changed over time, the more authentic it is. In the same way, oral legends and myths are relatively humbled by the much more prolific written and documented legends and myths, as if the latter was superior to the former.<br /> <br /> So, what do you do? How do you decide whether to go for the 'authentic' versions of a myth or the 'neo' versions of the myth? Or something else?<br /> <br /> And before you answer, consider this:<br /> <br /> Scion, as a game about myths and legends, is retelling those stories once again, which means it falls under the 'Neo' no matter how accurately they try to replicate everything. Because they are humans bound by the cycle of life and death.<br /> <br /> Just a thought.</div> </div> </div> </div>What counts as 'Neo' (using it in the context of 'newness' and not specifically neo-pagan religions) isn't really that cut and dry, since religions, as you noted, change over time, sometimes drastically... If any of the ancient Vedic era sages could see modern day Hinduism, their reactions would probably be somewhere along the lines of &quot;Why aren't we celebrating Indra... what is this Trimurti nonsense... why is Sarasvati doing Vak's job... and why the hell isn't everyone drunk... what do you mean you FORGOT what plant Soma was, we only based the entirety of our religiond offering it to the Gods!!!&quot;<br /> <br /> As for the oral vs written debate, that is a very deep seated issue in our own perceptions (and by extension the perceptions of many academics) that the written word is somehow more civilized and therefore worthy of more respect as a source than the spoken... hell, our modern day civilization pretty much runs on the understanding that until something is made out in writing, it is not legally binding.<br /> <br /> But let me give you an example of a deity with non traditional origins... in 1975 Bollywood released a very low budget film detailing the myths and legends of Santoshi, the Hindu Goddess of Satisfaction. It shows the story of her birth, showing how Ganesha's two sons desired a sister so Ganesha created Santoshi to satisfy them, and goes on to show her clashes with the Tridevi who do not take kindly to the new upstart. Devotees of Santoshi grew overnight with the film's immense popularity, and there are many temples and festivals in her honour. Only thing is... Santoshi did not exist before that movie. She is a Goddess born entirely of celluloid, not oral tradition or written scripture... does this make her any less Divine than her fellow Deva? Does this mean the mortals whop devotedly worship her to this day were duped by a Bollywood Producer? I don't think so, but that's a decision each gaming table will need to make itself.<br /> <br /> Hell, the 12th century east Indian poet Jayadeva Goswami once wanted to write a story about the childhood of Krishna, a deity who had already gained worship across India. In his tale, called the Gita Govinda, &quot;The Cowherd's Song&quot;, Jayadeva wrote especially of one particular milkmaid with whom Krishna was engaged in romance... Radha, who had not appeared as a character before this, but who would grow into one half of the Supreme Divinity for a goodly chunk of Hindus. The stories taht grew up around Radha are an excellent example of different cultures adapting religion to their own mindsets... Krishna's childhood home of Gokul is in the northern Indian state of Uttar Pradesh, and his future kingdom of Dwarka off the Western coast... but Radha's husband in the stories is named Ayan Ghosh, who is Krishna's uncle, and Ghosh is almost exclusively a surname found in the east of India... and she and her husband are worshipers of Kali, a Goddess primarily worshiped in the east. Hell, there are Hindus in Bengal that believe that Krishna is an Avatar not of Vishnu, but of Kali.<br /> <br /> Personally, I like to think that all Myths Are Real... but what that means, and how real is Real, is probably an exercise best left for each table to figure out.<br /> <br /> Basically, what I'm trying to say in that even cultures and religions that pride themselves on their unbroken lineage from the ancients are often muddied by outside influences and/or even internal change, so trying to figure out what is 'neo' is mostly only relevant in the game if you want it to be, and IMO not relevant at all to a real world person's belief systems unless they explicitly want to identify themselves as such.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1344,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","user":"Samudra","date":". 06-20-2017, 03:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra","name":"Samudra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1344_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"497"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T14:53:59-07:00","id":1105847,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Watcher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1105614-neo-versions?p=1105745#post1105745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I wrote up a whole post about this, but I actually realized I might be misunderstanding something. When you say that by retelling these stories they are 'Neo' in the sense that they are simply new, or 'Neo' in the sense of 'Neo-Paganism'? I realized the differentiation between those might be a tad confusing, but Neo-Paganism is <i>more</i> than simply being modern versions of those religions, they are revivals, reconstructions, and recontextualizations, not simply 'new' ones. (In fact, some Neo-Pagans do not like the suggestion that they are 'New' in any way, their belief being that they are 100% accurately following ancient beliefs.) The name can be very confusing if you are ESL, there is a whole <i>wild</i> history to the development of the Neo-Pagan movements, and the name can be the source of a lot of confusion.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well, to be perfectly honest, I feel that those differences lack substance. After all, the transformation of anything ultimately turns it in to something new. And, as humans are unable to be realize the ultimate truth and be 100% certain about anything(as Mohandas Ghandi famously said), I just don't think attempting to be 'exactly' like the ancients is possible or even desirable.<br /> As <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1344-samudra\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1344_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1344\" data-vbnamecard=\"1344\">Samudra</a> put it, myths and legends are full of muddy and messy contexts and influences. One of the things I like about Vedic and Hindu (and everything in between and outside of it in India) is just how messy and contradictory it can be, something I consider to be one of its strengths.<br /> <br /> But I am willing to change my mind about that as I readily admit I know very little about Neo-paganism in the European context.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>How can I know if what I claim I know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I might not know? How can I know if what I claim I don't know to be true is rejecting the idea that there is something I do know?<br /> -Zhuangzi","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1715-ostarion","name":"Ostarion","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1715_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"305"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-20T19:19:25-07:00","id":1105909,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ostarion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1105614-neo-versions?p=1105847#post1105847\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just don't think attempting to be 'exactly' like the ancients is possible or even desirable.</div> </div> </div> </div>We leave it up to the individual. When it comes to religion, people either place importance on exact references, or updated versions, at their preference... and either way works fine from a faith perspective (though not necessarily a historical perspective). I mean, people often DO say your beliefs are <u><b>badwrong</b></u>, even fighting wars and murdering each other over what you believe, but holy wars are generally frowned upon on Internet forums. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2273-incendax","name":"Incendax","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"283"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-21T19:59:39-07:00","id":1106390,"title":"","content":"Well, technically, you aren't &quot;Neo-Pagan&quot; unless you actually worship something. And people playing Scion aren't usually [I won't say always] doing it as worship - they are writing tales for fun. This is a game, not a faith - or did I miss something? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-21T22:20:28-07:00","id":1106420,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Purple Snit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1105614-neo-versions?p=1106390#post1106390\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, technically, you aren't &quot;Neo-Pagan&quot; unless you actually worship something. And people playing Scion aren't usually [I won't say always] doing it as worship - they are writing tales for fun. This is a game, not a faith - or did I miss something? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think the game is an act of worship, but I think some of the fans of the game are here at least in part because of their faith. I know I've dabbled into Egyptian paganism, and some others have as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-06-22T08:11:40-07:00","id":1106558,"title":"","content":"Oh, hey, I wasn't questioning individual faiths, just saying &quot;playing a game isn't technically what makes you a Pagan&quot;, neo- or otherwise. \u263a. I'm an atheist, and I enjoy the hell out of writing Scion-style games.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}